def populate(add):
 add(49,'Run a useful card sort',[
 ('Discover how people group content','Card sorting asks participants to organise representative topics. It reveals grouping expectations and vocabulary; it does not directly prove that people can find content in the resulting interface.'),
 ('Prepare and pilot','Define the research question, recruit relevant participants and create understandable, representative cards. Pilot the instructions and workload. The current NN/g article suggests roughly 30–50 cards; adjust scope to the study rather than treating this as a requirement.'),
 ('Run and interpret','Let people sort without teaching your preferred structure. Capture their labels, uncertainty and reasoning. Review agreement, differences and outliers alongside content constraints before proposing an IA.')],
 'Cedar includes course prices, joining instructions, venue access, refunds and tutor profiles. An open sort reveals that some participants group refunds with bookings rather than with membership. That expectation needs investigation, not automatic acceptance.',
 'Create twelve practice cards from Cedar’s content. Ask someone to group and label them without suggesting categories. Record one surprising grouping and their explanation.',
 'Your twelve-card activity is a learning exercise, not a validated research sample. Keep the original grouping and the participant’s words. Propose a structural change and a later tree-test task to check it.')
 add(50,'Choose open, closed or hybrid sorting',[
 ('Use open sorting to explore','In an open sort, participants create and name groups. Use it when you need to learn possible organisational schemes and language rather than evaluate only your existing categories.'),
 ('Use closed sorting to examine a proposal','A closed sort supplies categories and asks participants to place cards within them. It helps investigate whether items and labels fit people’s expectations, but does not test an entire navigation journey.'),
 ('Make hybrid rules explicit','A hybrid approach permits predefined groups and additional groups. Explain what participants may change, and distinguish evidence about existing categories from new proposals.')],
 'Cedar has not agreed on its course-support categories, so an open sort can explore expectations. Later, a closed sort can examine whether cancellation, payment and preparation topics fit proposed category names.',
 'Choose a sorting approach for a new site, a disputed label and an established taxonomy with possible gaps. Explain what you would still need to test afterwards.',
 'Open sorting can explore a new structure; closed sorting can investigate a proposal; hybrid sorting can reveal gaps while preserving a starting scheme. None establishes visual menu usability. Use task-based evaluation for finding and interaction.')
 add(51,'Prevent keyword matching from masquerading as insight',[
 ('Test concepts, not repeated words','Participants may group cards by matching words rather than by understanding the content. Repeated department names or prefixes can manufacture agreement.'),
 ('Write neutral descriptions','Use clear, distinct wording that describes each item’s meaning. Remove accidental verbal patterns without making the cards cryptic or changing what they represent.'),
 ('Inspect the reasoning','Pilot cards for comprehension and ask why people grouped them. Revise ambiguous wording before interpreting large amounts of sorting data.')],
 '“Member prices”, “Member conduct” and “Member stories” share a prefix but support different needs. Cedar can describe them as “Membership fees”, “Rules for using facilities” and “Experiences shared by members” to reduce superficial matching.',
 'Rewrite six cards that begin with a repeated organisational term. Ask a participant to explain one group. Could the new wording still be steering the answer?',
 'Removing a prefix alone is insufficient if the replacement adds an obvious category cue. Check equivalent detail and neutral phrasing across cards. Record whether the participant discusses meaning or simply repeats a shared word.')
 add(52,'Plan the participant sample',[
 ('Treat sample size as a decision','NN/g’s older card-sorting article recommends about 15 participants for many projects and more for larger studies. This is contextual guidance from limited evidence, not a guarantee or a universal minimum for every research question.'),
 ('Represent relevant differences','Recruit people who reflect the intended users and meaningful knowledge differences. A large convenience sample cannot repair recruitment that excludes an important audience.'),
 ('Separate exploration from measurement','Small moderated studies can reveal reasons and issues. Stable quantitative comparisons need a suitable sample and analysis plan. Explain uncertainty and avoid presenting a few responses as population facts.')],
 'A Cedar study with fifteen staff members may reveal staff vocabulary but miss newcomer expectations. Recruiting new and returning members addresses a different problem from merely increasing the total number.',
 'Write a recruitment plan naming the audience, important differences, recruitment route, available sample and limitations. What would you do if only five people were available?',
 'Use a small sample for exploratory learning, retain disagreements and plan follow-up. Do not report “80% of all members prefer this” from four of five participants. Distinguish a pilot from a reliable estimate.')
 add(53,'Reduce sampling and material bias',[
 ('Represent the content fairly','The cards you select shape the apparent categories. Overrepresenting one subject can make it appear more important or more subdivided than the real collection.'),
 ('Check selection rules','Define the inventory and how cards represent it. Include important tasks, less common items and likely outliers without loading the study to support a stakeholder’s desired answer.'),
 ('Pilot the whole experience','Review wording, card order, instructions and facilitation. Avoid coaching, approval signals and category hints. Record exclusions so others understand the study’s limits.')],
 'Cedar selects twenty arts cards but only one swimming card, although both represent similar shares of the offering. The results may split Arts into many groups while concealing distinctions within Swimming.',
 'Audit your practice card set. List included content types, excluded topics and the reason for each. Identify one imbalance that could change the result.',
 'A defensible selection reflects the research question and content space. Proportionate sampling is a starting point, not a rule that rare but critical tasks must disappear. Document deliberate oversampling and analyse it accordingly.')
 add(54,'Interpret clusters and dendrograms',[
 ('Read a cluster as evidence','A dendrogram visualises how items cluster under an analysis method. It summarises patterns of grouping; it is not an automatically correct sitemap.'),
 ('Compare candidate boundaries','Inspect which items consistently belong together and which move between groups. The merge scale and algorithm depend on the tool, so read its definitions before interpreting a cut or distance.'),
 ('Combine numbers with meaning','Review participant labels, comments, outliers and content needs. Different defensible cuts can produce different numbers of categories. Validate the proposed structure through finding tasks.')],
 'In a fictional Cedar sort, Prices and Concessions are grouped together by 18 of 20 participants; Refunds joins them in only 8. The stronger pair suggests a Fees cluster, while Refunds needs further investigation.',
 'Use this small similarity table: A with B: 90%; A with C: 35%; B with C: 40%. Propose a group and one uncertainty. What information is missing?',
 'A and B are plausible companions. The table alone does not name the group or explain C’s role. You need item meanings, sample details, other relationships and participant reasoning. A visual cluster does not eliminate design judgement.')
 add(55,'Use tree testing to check findability',[
 ('Evaluate the proposed hierarchy','A tree test presents a stripped-down category structure and asks people where they would find something. It isolates naming and grouping from visual interface design.'),
 ('Choose the question carefully','Use tasks that expose disputed categories and important destinations. Define acceptable locations in advance, including justified alternate paths.'),
 ('Know what remains untested','A good tree result does not establish that a real menu is visible, operable or accessible. Test the interface separately after testing the structure.')],
 'Cedar asks where a member would find the rules for recovering a payment after cancelling a class. The participant chooses among labels without seeing promotional cards, page styling or search.',
 'Convert one uncertainty from your card sort into a tree-test task. Describe the acceptable destination without copying the category name into the task.',
 '“You cannot attend a booked class. Where would you check whether your payment can be returned?” avoids directly saying “Refund policy”. Keep the scenario short and use language participants understand.')
 add(56,'Prepare, pilot and run a tree test',[
 ('Define the tree and answers','Include relevant categories down to the task destinations. Decide what counts as success before collecting results. A shallow tree can hide lower-level problems.'),
 ('Write unbiased tasks','Select common tasks, important risks and areas of disagreement. Avoid giving away category labels or burying the goal in a long story. Pilot the tasks for comprehension.'),
 ('Choose the study mode','Moderated sessions can reveal reasoning; unmoderated studies can collect broader behavioural data. Decide recruitment, sample, task order, data exclusions and reporting before launch.')],
 'A Cedar test includes booking, cancellation, accessibility support and venue hire. Each task has an expected destination and accepted alternate routes. A pilot discovers that “concession” is misunderstood, so the task wording is revised.',
 'Write a mini protocol: research question, tree version, participants, three tasks, accepted destinations and exclusion rules. Pilot one task without explaining the intended answer.',
 'A complete protocol makes your decisions reproducible. Record wording changes after the pilot. Avoid changing success criteria after seeing results merely to improve the score. Follow with interface testing once the hierarchy is promising.')
 add(57,'Turn tree-test results into changes',[
 ('Separate success from effort','Success records acceptable final destinations. Directness concerns the route and backtracking; time adds another effort measure. A correct final answer can conceal considerable difficulty.'),
 ('Inspect pathways','Weak first choices suggest a top-level expectation problem. Good first choices followed by wrong destinations suggest trouble further down. Examine distributions and accepted alternate paths, not just one overall percentage.'),
 ('Compare fairly','Interpret results by task importance, baseline and uncertainty. Compare equivalent tasks and recruitment. A small numerical difference is not automatically meaningful; quantitative comparisons need an appropriate sample and analysis.')],
 'Fictional data: 20 participants try Cedar cancellation. 14 succeed; 8 succeed directly; 6 succeed after backtracking; 6 fail. That is 70% success and 40% direct success using all participants as denominator. State that denominator explicitly.',
 'Half the participants start under Membership, but refunds live under Bookings. Propose two competing explanations and a next test. Do not assume the most popular wrong route must become correct.',
 'The top label may be unclear, or visitors may reasonably view refunds as a membership issue. Inspect other task paths, interview evidence and category overlap. Try clearer labels or justified cross-listing, then retest. Tool definitions of directness can differ.')
 add(58,'Diagnose IA problems separately from interface problems',[
 ('Use four complementary methods','Tree testing evaluates paths through a hierarchy. Closed sorting examines category fit. Click testing examines selection in a visual design. Usability testing observes a fuller task in context.'),
 ('Compare evidence across methods','If a labelled hierarchy works but the real page fails, investigate visibility and interaction. If both fail, investigate structure and wording as well. Neither pattern proves a cause on its own.'),
 ('Choose the smallest useful next study','Start from an observed task failure and a specific hypothesis. Match the method to the uncertainty rather than running every method automatically.')],
 'Cedar visitors find accessibility information in a text tree but overlook its low-contrast link in the page footer. A visual click test and observed task can examine whether presentation explains the difference.',
 'Match a method to each question: Do these labels fit the topics? Which visible link looks right? Can someone complete booking? Can they find the destination in a stripped hierarchy?',
 'Use closed sorting, click testing, usability testing and tree testing respectively. Each answers part of the problem. For example, selecting a plausible first link does not establish successful end-to-end booking.')
